WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.944
Welcome back to Building Azure
Security, the Federal Modernization

2
00:00:03.944 --> 00:00:08.112
Workbook's developer workbook for
the Azure Security Engineering guide.

3
00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:11.526
This is Chapter 2: the
public exposure register.

4
00:00:11.526 --> 00:00:15.739
DDoS protection, the web application
firewall and origin lock-down

5
00:00:15.739 --> 00:00:19.275
can't be proved complete without
a list of what is exposed.

6
00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:23.937
So the list is code, it runs every
week, and it notices what is new.

7
00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:26.966
We'll start from the guide's
Resource Graph queries,

8
00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:31.148
built from Microsoft Learn's starter
sample, see what they leave to you,

9
00:00:31.148 --> 00:00:34.825
read the register and its rules,
then walk two labs a week apart.

10
00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:39.850
The chapter has been reviewed and
approved, and the code is illustrative.

11
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.112
Why a register first?

12
00:00:45.112 --> 00:00:48.500
Because the later steps can't
be proved complete without it.

13
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.080
By the end of this chapter you can build
the register the guide's Step 2 asks for:

14
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.091
one row per exposure with its
resource, type, public name,

15
00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:02.450
owner, DDoS state, and its WAF
and mode, regenerated every week.

16
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:07.243
Every ownerless row is flagged, every
new exposure since last week reported,

17
00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:12.112
every one that went away reported with
a reminder to remove its DNS record, and

18
00:01:12.112 --> 00:01:17.362
every resource Defender for Cloud calls
internet-exposed that the register lacks.

19
00:01:17.362 --> 00:01:18.794
It's for platform owners,

20
00:01:18.794 --> 00:01:22.662
workload owners and the developers
who keep the queries current.

21
00:01:22.662 --> 00:01:27.820
And the register is a map of the attack
surface: it stays inside the boundary.

22
00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:32.957
Here's the starting point.

23
00:01:32.957 --> 00:01:38.371
The guide builds four Resource Graph queries
from Microsoft Learn's starter sample

24
00:01:38.371 --> 00:01:42.082
that lists every public IP:
public IPs with their DDoS mode,

25
00:01:42.082 --> 00:01:46.489
the resource they're attached to and
an owner tag; Front Door profiles;

26
00:01:46.489 --> 00:01:49.350
application gateways
with their WAF policy;

27
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.371
and every resource whose public
network access isn't disabled.

28
00:01:53.371 --> 00:01:57.821
Learn recommends an inventory of
every public IP and its protection,

29
00:01:57.821 --> 00:02:03.208
kept with Resource Graph, and a quarterly
review of which IPs still need protection.

30
00:02:03.208 --> 00:02:08.230
And Learn's cost guidance adds that
fewer public IPs means less to protect.

31
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.867
What do four queries leave to you?

32
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:18.905
Four lists aren't a register, so the kit
joins them into one with eleven columns.

33
00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:22.546
A public IP's attachment
is an IP configuration ID,

34
00:02:22.546 --> 00:02:27.518
so the kit finds the gateway behind
it and that gateway's WAF and mode.

35
00:02:27.518 --> 00:02:31.799
A Front Door profile's public
names are its custom domains and

36
00:02:31.799 --> 00:02:36.080
its WAF lives in a security policy,
so both are read per profile.

37
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.949
Classic Front Door is public too,

38
00:02:38.949 --> 00:02:43.305
and retires on March 31,
2027, so it's in the register.

39
00:02:43.305 --> 00:02:47.905
Nothing compares with last week, so
the kit reports new and gone rows.

40
00:02:47.905 --> 00:02:51.505
And Defender for Cloud's view
is reconciled, not eyeballed.

41
00:02:51.505 --> 00:02:53.850
A test pins each answer.

42
00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.237
Here's what the register holds.

43
00:02:59.237 --> 00:03:02.928
The public name is a public
IP's DNS name and address,

44
00:03:02.928 --> 00:03:08.375
a Front Door profile's custom domains,
or an application gateway's public IP.

45
00:03:08.375 --> 00:03:10.238
The owner is the owner tag.

46
00:03:10.238 --> 00:03:14.276
The DDoS column is the IP's protection
mode, or "Front Door edge",

47
00:03:14.276 --> 00:03:19.688
because Learn says Front Door's edge has
built-in layer three and four protection.

48
00:03:19.688 --> 00:03:23.237
And the WAF columns name
the policy and its mode.

49
00:03:23.237 --> 00:03:26.717
Five rules read it: a row
without an owner is a finding;

50
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:29.630
a public IP attached to
nothing is a warning;

51
00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.300
a new row since last week is information;

52
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.023
a row that went away is a
warning to check its DNS records;

53
00:03:36.023 --> 00:03:41.720
and an exposure Defender reports that the
register lacks is a gap in the queries.

54
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.520
Who's involved?

55
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.122
The platform owner runs the register
every week, keeps it inside the boundary,

56
00:03:51.122 --> 00:03:54.358
and reviews it each quarter
with the security officer.

57
00:03:54.358 --> 00:03:57.038
Workload owners answer for their own rows:

58
00:03:57.038 --> 00:04:00.483
every row needs an owner tag,
and every finding a ticket.

59
00:04:00.483 --> 00:04:03.078
The security officer
reconciles the register

60
00:04:03.078 --> 00:04:05.807
with Defender for Cloud
at that quarterly review.

61
00:04:05.807 --> 00:04:09.220
The kit's reads need only Reader
on the intermediate root.

62
00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.400
Nothing in this chapter writes.

63
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.262
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

64
00:04:19.262 --> 00:04:22.279
The fixture estate is
read twice, a week apart,

65
00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:26.112
and a small file stands in for
Defender for Cloud's export.

66
00:04:26.112 --> 00:04:31.531
Step 1 builds the first week's register:
nineteen exposures, eight public IPs,

67
00:04:31.531 --> 00:04:35.316
three Front Door profiles,
two application gateways

68
00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:38.499
and six PaaS resources,
sixteen with an owner.

69
00:04:38.499 --> 00:04:39.850
Step 2 reads it.

70
00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:44.988
The application gateway's public IP
carries the gateway's WAF policy.

71
00:04:44.988 --> 00:04:47.941
The careers Front Door profile has no WAF,

72
00:04:47.941 --> 00:04:52.480
the legacy profile is classic,
and three rows have no owner.

73
00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:00.902
Step 3 reads the findings: three
exposures with no owner, a public IP

74
00:05:00.902 --> 00:05:06.168
attached to nothing, and one resource
Defender reports that no query finds.

75
00:05:06.168 --> 00:05:09.508
It's a container group with
a public address that isn't

76
00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:12.393
a public IP resource, so
it's a gap in the queries.

77
00:05:12.393 --> 00:05:15.302
Step 4 builds the register
again a week later,

78
00:05:15.302 --> 00:05:18.605
against the first one, and
step 5 reads what changed.

79
00:05:18.605 --> 00:05:21.680
One public IP is new, and has no owner.

80
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.870
The orphan is gone, with
the reminder to remove

81
00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.143
any DNS record that still
points at its address.

82
00:05:28.143 --> 00:05:30.970
Step 6 runs the chapter's tests.

83
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.270
Lab 2 moves to a test subscription
in a test tenant, never production.

84
00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.396
Sign in to commercial Azure,
write the settings file,

85
00:05:42.396 --> 00:05:45.958
and build the register live with
Defender for Cloud's export.

86
00:05:45.958 --> 00:05:50.745
The classic Front Door, gateway and WAF
queries aren't verified on Learn yet,

87
00:05:50.745 --> 00:05:53.757
so allow them and check
their rows in the portal.

88
00:05:53.757 --> 00:05:58.557
Then run the guide's own test: create a
public IP and build the register again.

89
00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:01.639
It should appear as new,
and as attached to nothing;

90
00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:04.570
Resource Graph can take
a few minutes to show it.

91
00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:08.820
Back out by deleting the public
IP and the output, and sign out.

92
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.190
The authors haven't run this lab.

93
00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.540
Each output is evidence for something
specific, from the guide's requirements table.

94
00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.627
The register itself, every
exposure with its owner,

95
00:06:22.627 --> 00:06:27.479
supports NIST's system component inventory
and boundary protection controls,

96
00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:31.228
and aligns to a FedRAMP cloud-native
architecture indicator.

97
00:06:31.228 --> 00:06:35.137
Ownerless and unattached exposures,
what changed since last week,

98
00:06:35.137 --> 00:06:40.114
and the reconciliation with Defender for
Cloud support the same inventory control.

99
00:06:40.114 --> 00:06:44.755
The kit's output supports those controls;
the assessor decides, and the agency

100
00:06:44.755 --> 00:06:49.890
lines its own evidence up with FedRAMP's
indicators rather than reporting them.

101
00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.903
What breaks, and what guards against it?

102
00:06:55.903 --> 00:07:00.666
Published in a public repository, the
register is a map of the attack surface,

103
00:07:00.666 --> 00:07:04.144
so the check prints a warning
every run and the runbook's

104
00:07:04.144 --> 00:07:08.152
preflight refuses an output folder
inside the kit's repository.

105
00:07:08.152 --> 00:07:11.350
Some public addresses
aren't public IP resources,

106
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:15.952
so the queries can't see them; Defender
for Cloud's export closes that gap.

107
00:07:15.952 --> 00:07:20.494
A DNS record left pointing at a
released address can be taken over,

108
00:07:20.494 --> 00:07:23.353
so every exposure that goes away is named.

109
00:07:23.353 --> 00:07:27.546
And an owner tag nobody checks names
a team that may no longer exist,

110
00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:30.490
which is what the quarterly review is for.

111
00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.590
Here's what done looks like.

112
00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:40.603
The register runs every week, and every
ownerless row has a ticket with an owner.

113
00:07:40.603 --> 00:07:45.677
Every exposure that went away had
its DNS records checked and removed.

114
00:07:45.677 --> 00:07:48.519
Defender for Cloud's
export was reconciled,

115
00:07:48.519 --> 00:07:52.228
and every gap is either added
to the queries or explained.

116
00:07:52.228 --> 00:07:55.991
And the register and its run
folders live inside the boundary.

117
00:07:55.991 --> 00:08:01.910
Next, Chapter 3 traces each public IP in
this register to its DDoS protection.
